---
/**
 * Privacy. Short, accurate, and written to match what this site actually does.
 *
 * UPDATED 24 Sep 2026, when GA4 and the Meta pixel were carried over from the
 * legacy site for the cutover. The earlier version promised that this page would
 * name any analytics "before it ships" — this is that promise being kept, and it
 * is why the page names the pixel in plain words instead of calling it
 * "marketing technologies". A privacy page that describes a site you no longer
 * run is worse than none.
 *
 * Anything added to BUSINESS.analytics or BUSINESS.chatWidget has to land here
 * in the same commit.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { BUSINESS } from '../data/business';
import { buildTitle } from '../lib/seo';

export const prerender = true;
const updated = '2026-09-24';

const schema = {
  path: '/privacy/',
  title: buildTitle('Privacy'),
  description: 'What King of Kings Window Cleaning collects on this website, what it does not collect, and how to ask us about the information we hold.',
  image: '/brand/king-of-kings-social.jpg',
  imageAlt: 'Privacy information for King of Kings Window Cleaning',
  crumbs: [{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'Privacy', path: '/privacy/' }],
  faqs: [],
};
---
<BaseLayout schema={schema} noindex={true}>
  <article class="t1 prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>

    <h2>What this site collects</h2>
    <p>
      This website is a set of static pages. It has no login, cart or account, and
      the pages themselves collect nothing about you. Three third-party services
      do run on it, and each is named below: a chat widget, Google Analytics, and
      a Meta advertising pixel.
    </p>

    <h2>Google Analytics</h2>
    <p>
      We use Google Analytics 4 to see how many people visit, which pages get read
      and roughly where they arrived from — a search, a link, an advert. It sets
      its own cookies in your browser and sends Google your IP address, which
      Google uses to work out an approximate location and then discards rather
      than storing.
    </p>
    <p>
      We read it in aggregate. It tells us a page was read, not who read it, and
      we have no way to tie it to you personally unless you separately contact us.
    </p>

    <h2>The Meta advertising pixel</h2>
    <p>
      We advertise on Facebook and Instagram, and this site carries Meta's pixel
      so we can tell which adverts lead to someone actually looking at the work.
      It reports your visit to Meta, and if you have a Facebook or Instagram
      account Meta can connect that visit to it. It may also be used to show you
      our adverts afterwards.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is the one thing on this site that is here for our benefit rather than
      yours, so we would rather say so plainly than bury it. You can limit it in
      your Meta account's ad settings, and a tracker blocker stops it here.
    </p>

    <h2>Turning all of it off</h2>
    <p>
      None of the three loads before the page has rendered, and none of them is
      needed for the site to work — every page is readable with JavaScript turned
      off completely. Blocking third-party scripts, or using a browser that does
      it for you, stops all three without breaking anything you came here to read.
    </p>

    <h2>The chat widget</h2>
    <p>
      Most pages carry a chat widget provided by LeadConnector, which is the
      messaging tool behind our customer relationship system. It loads after the
      page has rendered — when you first scroll, tap or type, or shortly after if
      you do none of those — rather than immediately. It is on the contact page,
      where it is the quote form, and not on the guarantee or privacy pages.
    </p>
    <p>
      When it loads it runs LeadConnector's own code and will set its own storage
      in your browser so a conversation survives a page change. If you start a
      chat, what you type goes to LeadConnector and to us, and we use it to answer
      you and to quote the work. LeadConnector processes it under its own terms as
      our provider.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you would rather not load it, it will not start on its own before you
      interact with the page, and blocking third-party scripts in your browser
      stops it entirely — the rest of the site works without it, because nothing
      on these pages depends on JavaScript.
    </p>

    <h2>What happens when you contact us</h2>
    <p>
      If you call, email, or submit a quote request, we keep what you send us —
      your name, contact details, the address of the property and what you have
      asked about — so we can quote and carry out the work. We use it for that and
      for following up about it. We do not sell it.
    </p>

    <h2>Hosting</h2>
    <p>
      The site is served by our hosting provider, which keeps standard server logs
      including IP addresses for security and operational purposes. That is the
      provider's processing, not ours, and it is the ordinary behaviour of any web
      server.
    </p>

    <h2>Third-party services</h2>
    <p>
      Where we link out — to our Google Business Profile, to a review platform, to
      a quote form hosted elsewhere — that service has its own privacy terms and
      its own cookies, and following the link puts you under those rather than
      these. Analytics and the advertising pixel
      are named above, which is the promise this page previously made about them.
    </p>

    <h2>Asking about your information</h2>
    <p>
      Email <a href={`mailto:${BUSINESS.email}`}>{BUSINESS.email}</a> or call{' '}
      <a href={`tel:${BUSINESS.phoneE164}`}>{BUSINESS.phone}</a> and ask. We will
      tell you what we hold and remove it if you want it removed.
    </p>

    <p class="updated">Last updated <time datetime={updated}>{updated}</time>.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
